{:edb/db-before-t 2470 :edb/db-after-t 2471 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535783 50 #inst "2026-10-09T07:09:23.208Z" 13194139535783 true] [17592186110263 1100 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(\\mu, 6^2)$ with $P(X > 4) = 0.1841$, find $\\mu$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7580 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7611 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7642 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7881 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7910 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7939 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8159 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8186 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8212 \\,$ |" 13194139535783 false] [17592186110263 1100 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(\\mu, 6^2)$ with $P(X > 4) = 0.1841,$ find $\\mu.$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7580 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7611 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7642 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7881 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7910 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7939 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8159 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8186 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8212 \\,$ |" 13194139535783 true] [17592186110263 1145 "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 4) &= 1 - P(X > 4) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.1841 \\\\\n&= 0.8159\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 4) &= 0.8159 \\\\\nP(Z \\le \\frac{4 - μ}{6}) &= 0.8159 \\\\\nΦ(\\frac{4 - μ}{6}) &= 0.8159 \\\\\n\\frac{4 - μ}{6} &= Φ^{-1}(0.8159)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.90) = 0.8159 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(0.8159) = 0.90\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4 - μ}{6} &= 0.90 \\\\\n4 - μ &= 5.4 \\\\\nμ &= -1.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535783 false] [17592186110263 1145 "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =1-P(X>4) \\\\  & =1-0.1841 \\\\  & =0.8159\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =0.8159 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{4-\\mu }{6}) & =0.8159 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{4-\\mu }{6}) & =0.8159 \\\\ \\frac{4-\\mu }{6} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8159)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.90) = 0.8159 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.8159) = 0.90.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4-\\mu }{6} & =0.90 \\\\ 4-\\mu  & =5.4 \\\\ \\mu  & =-1.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535783 true] [17592186110264 1100 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X\\sim N(2.5, \\sigma^2)$ and $P(X \\geq 3) = 0.4013$, find $\\sigma^2$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5910 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5948 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5987 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6293 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6331 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6368 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6700 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6736 \\,$ |" 13194139535783 false] [17592186110264 1100 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X\\sim N(2.5, \\sigma^2)$ and $P(X \\geq 3) = 0.4013,$ find $\\sigma^2.$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5910 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5948 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5987 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6293 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6331 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6368 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6700 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6736 \\,$ |" 13194139535783 true] [17592186110264 1145 "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 3) &= 1 - P(X \\ge 3) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.4013 \\\\\n&= 0.5987\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 3) &= 0.5987 \\\\\nP(Z \\le \\frac{3 - 2.5}{σ}) &= 0.5987 \\\\\nΦ(\\frac{0.5}{σ}) &= 0.5987 \\\\\n\\frac{0.5}{σ} &= Φ^{-1}(0.5987)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.25) = 0.5987 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(0.5987) = 0.25\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{0.5}{σ} &= 0.25 \\\\\nσ &= \\frac{0.5}{0.25} \\\\\nσ &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nσ^{2} = 2^{2} = 4\n$$" 13194139535783 false] [17592186110264 1145 "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =1-P(X\\ge 3) \\\\  & =1-0.4013 \\\\  & =0.5987\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =0.5987 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{3-2.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.5987 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.5987 \\\\ \\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.5987)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.25) = 0.5987 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.5987) = 0.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & =0.25 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{0.5}{0.25} \\\\ \\sigma  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}={2}^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535783 true] [17592186110265 1100 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(0, \\sigma^2)$ is a normal random variable with $P(-3.18 <        X <        3.18) = 0.4038,$ find $\\sigma.$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6628 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6700 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6985 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7324 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ |" 13194139535783 true] [17592186110265 1100 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(0, \\sigma^2)$ is a normal random variable with $P(-3.18 < X < 3.18) = 0.4038$, find $\\sigma$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6628 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6700 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6985 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7324 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ |" 13194139535783 false] [17592186110265 1145 "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-3.18 < X < 3.18) &= 0.4038 \\\\\n2P(0 < X < 3.18) &= 0.4038 \\\\\nP(0 < X < 3.18) &= 0.2019 \\\\\nP(X < 3.18) - P(X < 0) &= 0.2019 \\\\\nP(X < 3.18) - 0.5 &= 0.2019 \\\\\nP(X < 3.18) &= 0.7019 \\\\\nP(X \\le 3.18) &= 0.7019\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 3.18) &= 0.7019 \\\\\nP(Z \\le \\frac{3.18 - 0}{σ}) &= 0.7019 \\\\\nΦ(\\frac{3.18}{σ}) &= 0.7019 \\\\\n\\frac{3.18}{σ} &= Φ^{-1}(0.7019)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.53) = 0.7019 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(0.7019) = 0.53\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3.18}{σ} &= 0.53 \\\\\nσ &= \\frac{3.18}{0.53} \\\\\nσ &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535783 false] [17592186110265 1145 "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-3.18<X<3.18) & =0.4038 \\\\ 2P(0<X<3.18) & =0.4038 \\\\ P(0<X<3.18) & =0.2019 \\\\ P(X<3.18)-P(X<0) & =0.2019 \\\\ P(X<3.18)-0.5 & =0.2019 \\\\ P(X<3.18) & =0.7019 \\\\ P(X\\le 3.18) & =0.7019\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3.18) & =0.7019 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{3.18-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.7019 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{3.18}{\\sigma }) & =0.7019 \\\\ \\frac{3.18}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7019)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.53) = 0.7019 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.7019) = 0.53.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3.18}{\\sigma } & =0.53 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3.18}{0.53} \\\\ \\sigma  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535783 true] [17592186110266 1100 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(\\mu, 2^2)$ is a normal random variable with $P( -4\\mu<             X <        6\\mu) = 0.6528,$ find $\\mu$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7673 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7704 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7967 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7995 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8023 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8238 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8264 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8289 \\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-4\\mu<             X <        6\\mu$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu.$*" 13194139535783 true] [17592186110266 1100 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(\\mu, 2^2)$ is a normal random variable with $P(-4\\mu< X < 6\\mu) = 0.6528$, find $\\mu$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7673 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7704 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7967 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7995 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8023 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8238 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8264 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8289 \\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-4\\mu< X < 6\\mu$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu$.*" 13194139535783 false] [17592186110266 1145 "Note that we can write the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(\\mu -5\\mu <     X <     \\mu +5\\mu) = 0.6528,\n$$\n\nwhich tells us that the interval $-4\\mu<             X <       6\\mu$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu.$\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mu -5\\mu <X<\\mu +5\\mu ) & =0.6528 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<\\mu +5\\mu ) & =0.6528 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<\\mu +5\\mu ) & =0.3264 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +5\\mu )-P(X<\\mu ) & =0.3264 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +5\\mu )-0.5 & =0.3264 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +5\\mu ) & =0.8264 \\\\ P(X\\le 6\\mu ) & =0.8264\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6\\mu ) & =0.8264 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{6\\mu -\\mu }{2}) & =0.8264 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{5\\mu }{2}) & =0.8264 \\\\ \\frac{5\\mu }{2} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8264)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.94) = 0.8264 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.8264) = 0.94.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\mu }{2} & =0.94 \\\\ 5\\mu  & =1.88 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈0.38\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535783 true] [17592186110266 1145 "Note that we can write the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(\\mu -5\\mu < X < \\mu +5\\mu) = 0.6528\n$$\n\nwhich tells us that the interval $-4\\mu< X < 6\\mu$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu$.\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(μ - 5μ < X < μ + 5μ) &= 0.6528 \\\\\n2P(μ < X < μ + 5μ) &= 0.6528 \\\\\nP(μ < X < μ + 5μ) &= 0.3264 \\\\\nP(X < μ + 5μ) - P(X < μ) &= 0.3264 \\\\\nP(X < μ + 5μ) - 0.5 &= 0.3264 \\\\\nP(X < μ + 5μ) &= 0.8264 \\\\\nP(X \\le 6μ) &= 0.8264\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 6μ) &= 0.8264 \\\\\nP(Z \\le \\frac{6μ - μ}{2}) &= 0.8264 \\\\\nΦ(\\frac{5μ}{2}) &= 0.8264 \\\\\n\\frac{5μ}{2} &= Φ^{-1}(0.8264)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.94) = 0.8264 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(0.8264) = 0.94\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5μ}{2} &= 0.94 \\\\\n5μ &= 1.88 \\\\\n&= μ \\mid \\approx 0.38\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535783 false] [17592186110490 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366077 13194139535783 true] [17592186110490 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366084 13194139535783 true] [17592186110490 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366112 13194139535783 true] [17592186110490 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366130 13194139535783 true] [17592186110490 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366151 13194139535783 true] [17592186110491 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366134 13194139535783 true] [17592186110491 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366138 13194139535783 true] [17592186110491 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366141 13194139535783 true] [17592186110491 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366158 13194139535783 true] [17592186110491 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366165 13194139535783 true] [17592186110492 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366105 13194139535783 true] [17592186110492 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366115 13194139535783 true] [17592186110492 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366119 13194139535783 true] [17592186110492 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366145 13194139535783 true] [17592186110493 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366091 13194139535783 true] [17592186110493 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366098 13194139535783 true] [17592186110493 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366123 13194139535783 true] [17592186110493 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366127 13194139535783 true] [17592186110493 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366148 13194139535783 true] [17592186111350 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535783 true] [17592186111350 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0, σ^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(\\mid X \\mid < 9) = 0.5468$, find $σ^{2}$.\n\n| $z$ | $0.04$ | $0.05$ | $0.06$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.7$ | $0.7704$ | $0.7734$ | $0.7764$ |\n| $0.8$ | $0.7995$ | $0.8023$ | $0.8051$ |\n| $0.9$ | $0.8264$ | $0.8289$ | $0.8315$ |" 13194139535783 false] [17592186111350 1100 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(|X|<9)=0.5468,$ find ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ \n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ | $\\,0.8051\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8264\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ | $\\,0.8315\\,$ |" 13194139535783 true] [17592186111350 1145 "First, we express the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(|X|<9)=P(-9<X<9).\n$$\n\nThen, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-9<X<9) & =0.5468 \\\\ 2P(0<X<9) & =0.5468 \\\\ P(0<X<9) & =0.2374 \\\\ P(X<9)-P(X<0) & =0.2734 \\\\ P(X<9)-0.5 & =0.2734 \\\\ P(X<9) & =0.7734 \\\\ P(X\\le 9) & =0.7734\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 9) & =0.7734 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{9-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.7734 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{9}{\\sigma }) & =0.7734 \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7734)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.75)=0.7734\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7734)=0.75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{\\sigma } & =0.75 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{9}{0.75} \\\\ \\sigma  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =(12)^{2}=144.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535783 true] [17592186366077 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366078 13194139535783 true] [17592186366077 1089 "q-132145" 13194139535783 true] [17592186366077 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535783 true] [17592186366077 1100 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{5}^{2})$ with $P(X\\ge -1)=0.5793,$ find $\\mu .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4207\\,$ | $\\,0.4168\\,$ | $\\,0.4129\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4602\\,$ | $\\,0.4562\\,$ | $\\,0.4522\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.5000\\,$ | $\\,0.4960\\,$ | $\\,0.4920\\,$ |" 13194139535783 true] [17592186366077 1113 #uuid "5a25085a-365b-5ddc-b16d-221db979b7e8" 13194139535783 true] [17592186366077 1145 "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le -1) & =P(X<-1) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\ge -1) \\\\  & =1-0.5793 \\\\  & =0.4207\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le -1) & =0.4207 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{-1-\\mu }{5}) & =0.4207 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{-1-\\mu }{5}) & =0.4207 \\\\ \\frac{-1-\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.4207)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.2)=0.4207\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.4207)=-0.2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-1-\\mu }{5} & =-0.2 \\\\ -1-\\mu  & =-1 \\\\ \\mu  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535783 true] [17592186366078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366079 13194139535783 true] [17592186366078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366080 13194139535783 true] [17592186366078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366081 13194139535783 true] [17592186366078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366082 13194139535783 true] [17592186366078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366083 13194139535783 true] [17592186366078 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535783 true] [17592186366078 1041 "selection" 13194139535783 true] [17592186366078 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366082 13194139535783 true] [17592186366078 1110 #uuid "1e7cf02d-a6cc-593c-82e5-aa24c4fd5436" 13194139535783 true] [1759218636